+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a value like `90m` or `1h30m` into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is
+either a whole number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of a whole number followed by a unit. The units
+are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may be used at most once, in that order,
+without spaces, so `1d2h3m4s` is valid but `30m1h` and `1h 30m` are not. Units are case-insensitive. Negative and
+decimal values are not accepted.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
